A||||3||Jason Kamrud||Moroso oil filter adapter for V-8||||1||
Z||000000||Jason Kamrud||09-26-2001||10:14 PM||||I'm finally to the point where I am lining up parts for a V-8 swap. I need to relocate the oil filter so I can put the starter on the right side of the engine. Morosso's part #23770 is for the spin-on filters, I assume, but my '64 block came factory with the canister filter. I could put an adapter to a spin-on type on it, but that would stick out about an inch from the base, defeating the purpose.  What are my options??<P>------------------<BR>Jason Kamrud||||reg||1
Z||000001||JasonS||09-27-2001||04:29 PM||||I'm not sure what your block looks like, but I'm pretty sure they're all about the same.  I have an early '80s block that after I had rebuilt, I had to take the threaded screw that the oil filter spins on, out.  Once you do that, it's just a matter of bolting the moroso adapter on the block. It takes a lot of trial fitting to get it worked out.  I did a lot of grinding on my starter to get the 90 degree fittings on the adapter to fit. There are a couple of ways to do it and if you need some more help, I'll be glad to offer it in any way that I can.  Good luck,<P>Jason ||||reg||6
Z||000002||theogre||09-27-2001||08:07 PM||||He has a canister filter... it's much diferant than the spin on jobs most people are used to. The closest equivalent I can give you for an example would be the 4 cylinders where the filter is in the oil pan. In his case it's in a heavy steel can that is held, if I remember right, with a long bolt...<P>The only thing I think you are going to be able to do on that is find someone with a lathe that can make one from a chunk of billit aluminum. The holes for the hoses are then drilled and tapped as needed. Time consuming but not rocket science. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Butter||09-28-2001||04:55 PM||||Jason,<BR>I run an old 283 cid that had the canaster filter like you speak of.  I bought an adapter but did not like the way it worked.  <BR>What I did was make a small plate that fit up in where part of the canaster attachment was, drilled two holes for the bolts to hold it on and made my own gasket.  The bolts I used fit very close to the inside lip but it wasn't hard to tighten with a wrench.  Then I tapped the block and used clamping hose nipples for my remote oil filter.  I used a pipe extention for the nipple next to the starter.||||reg||10
